 - No step-3 optimization is proposed: the fixture's phases are microseconds and say nothing about which phase dominates in production. Run the profiler on the saved q50 evidence and read `solver_measurement.phases` first.
 - Suggested next action: independently review `2b482dd..5b9a3e9`. Repair concrete findings, then run one catalog qualification/adoption pass locally, verify implicit catalog selection, and perform the planned demand re-warm.
 - Step 3 review fixes and optimisation, 2026-09-13 (local machine, real evidence): the two required corrections landed as `7e1a976`. `_SOLVER_PHASE_OBSERVER` is a `contextvars.ContextVar` set and reset through a token in `finally`; the added interleaving test fails on the old module global with `KeyError: 'milp_solve'`, which is the phase being recorded into the other profile's collector, and passes on the fix. `passage_solver.py` is now inside `replay_source_sha256()`, so a replay contract can no longer claim the same source identity after the file that decides the solver cache key, the feasibility check and the solve call has changed.
-- The optimisation is a call-local memo in `departure_bound_constraints`, keyed by the VALUE of `(departure quarter, option.edges)` and returning the exact tuple of bound rows. Nothing global, nothing path-derived, nothing survives the call. Append order is safe because `coo_matrix.tocsr()` canonicalises -- duplicates summed, indices sorted -- verified directly on scipy 1.13.1, so reusing the first option's set-iteration order reproduces the same arrays. `option.edges` is a tuple everywhere in production: `departure_reconciliation` parses it with `tuple(...)` and `_shifted_alternatives` carries the same object through `replace()`, which is also why the memo hits so often.
-- Measured A/B/B/A on the saved 2027-06-25 q50 evidence, 3 repeats per profile, one empty solver cache per profile and never shared between revisions (the key hashes the source on purpose). Warm `departure_bound_constraints` median 0.400201 -> 0.192282 s, 51.95% faster; the worst candidate measurement is still 51.61% below the best baseline one, so the distributions do not overlap. Warm `fit_integer_flows` median 0.762698 -> 0.575360 s, 24.56% faster. Cold fit is unchanged at +0.59% because it is ~3 s of MILP. Requirement was 15% on the phase and no regression on the total.
+- The optimisation is a call-local memo in `departure_bound_constraints`, keyed by the VALUE of `(departure quarter, option.edges)` and returning the exact tuple of bound rows. Nothing global, nothing path-derived, nothing survives the call. Append order is safe because `coo_matrix.tocsr()` canonicalises -- duplicates summed, indices sorted -- verified directly on scipy 1.13.1, so reusing the first option's set-iteration order reproduces the same arrays. `option.edges` is a tuple on the production construction path: `departure_reconciliation` parses it with `tuple(...)` and `_shifted_alternatives` carries the same object through `replace()`, which is also why the memo hits so often. The public helper still accepts list-backed `RouteDeparture` values; the memo normalises only non-tuples so that contract is preserved without adding iterations for tuple subclasses.
+- Measured A/B/B/A on the saved 2027-06-25 q50 evidence, 3 repeats per profile, one empty solver cache per profile and never shared between revisions (the key hashes the source on purpose). Warm `departure_bound_constraints` median 0.400201 -> 0.192282 s, 51.95% faster; the worst candidate measurement is still 51.61% below the best baseline one, so the distributions do not overlap. Warm `fit_integer_flows` median 0.762698 -> 0.575360 s, 24.56% faster. Cold fit is practically unchanged; the candidate median is 0.59% faster because the ~3 s MILP dominates. Requirement was 15% on the phase and no regression on the total.
 - Exactness: all 11 solver request arrays (`c`, `data`, `indices`, `indptr`, `integrality`, `lower`, `options`, `row_lower`, `row_upper`, `shape`, `upper`) are identical across every baseline/candidate repeat pairing, `request.npz` is byte-identical in all four arms, published routes/agents/selection are byte-identical, and `selection_reproduced.state` is `identical` in all 12 replays. Unit exactness is pinned against a frozen verbatim copy of the pre-change implementation over repeated routes, repeated edges, repeated sensor visits, mixed measured/unmeasured edges, absent bounds, empty rows, zero bounds, negative departures, beyond-window departures and 12 seeded random systems. Two of the new tests were genuinely red first: they count route walks and demanded 1 and 2 where the old code did 5 and 3.
 - Evidence: `validation/passage_step3_departure_bounds_experiment_20260913.json`, release_evidence false. Tests: focused 216 passed, wider dependent set 370 passed with the one pre-existing urllib3 LibreSSL warning.
 - Workspace note: this was done in a git worktree at `/private/tmp/gs-step3-departure-bounds`, with the baseline arm in a second worktree at `7e1a976`, because the main checkout carries 115 unrelated uncommitted changes on `strict-sensor-routes-2026-09-01` that must not travel onto this branch. A fresh worktree has no gitignored `sumo/`, so `direction_split.json`, `net.net.xml` and `plain.edg.xml` were copied in read-only; without them `test_q50_subset_identity` and `test_independent_daily` fail on missing generated inputs, which is environment, not code.
